---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site } from '../config';
---

<Base title="About — Vansh">
  <section class="shell page-section about-page">
    <div class="eyebrow">BEHIND THE NOTEBOOK</div>

    <h1 class="page-title">
      Hi, I’m Vansh<span class="accent">.</span>
    </h1>

    <p class="about-lead">
      A mechanical engineering student, aerospace enthusiast, and Linux user
      who enjoys understanding how things work, building things, and finding
      ways to make them better.
    </p>

    <div class="about-columns">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Engineering, with curiosity.</h2>

        <p>
          I’m a mechanical engineering student at Hofstra University,
          concentrating in aerospace and graduating in 2027. I also serve
          as co-president of our AIAA chapter, helping create opportunities
          for students to explore aerospace through projects, workshops,
          and connections beyond the classroom.
        </p>

        <p>
          Aircraft design, rocketry, and simulation are particular interests
          of mine. I’m drawn to the process of taking a question, working
          through the physics, and developing something that can be modeled,
          built, or tested.
        </p>

        <h2>From drawings to the shop floor.</h2>

        <p>
          I spent six months as an Aerospace Manufacturing Engineer Co-op
          at Arkwin Industries, working with aerospace hydraulic actuators
          and the tooling and processes used to manufacture them.
          My work included inspection fixtures, CNC workholding, custom
          cutting tools, engineering drawings, and manufacturing documentation.
        </p>

        <p>
          That experience gave me a better appreciation for the details
          connecting a design to a working part: tolerances, inspection,
          manufacturability, and clear instructions. A model on a screen
          becomes much more interesting when you have to figure out how
          someone will actually make and measure it.
        </p>

        <p>
          I also worked as a research intern in Hofstra’s ideaHUb makerspace,
          supporting prototyping, mechanical testing, troubleshooting,
          and equipment guides. Other projects have included a low-cost
          below-knee prosthetic leg and modifications that make children’s
          toys more accessible to users with limited mobility.
        </p>

        <h2>Linux, hardware, and open technology.</h2>

        <p>
          My interest in technology extends well beyond engineering software.
          I use Fedora Linux on my personal computer and enjoy exploring
          the desktop ecosystem, open-source applications, and the details
          that make a system feel good to use.
        </p>

        <p>
          That often turns into experimenting with software, troubleshooting
          hardware, comparing alternatives, or trying to understand why
          something behaves the way it does. I’m interested in performance,
          usability, repairability, and how much control a tool gives
          the person using it.
        </p>

        <h2>Learning to build with code and AI.</h2>

        <p>
          I use Python and MATLAB as engineering tools, and I’m also
          dabbling in AI-assisted programming to explore ideas, modify
          software, and build small tools. I’m still developing those
          skills, and part of the appeal is learning how the pieces
          fit together as I go.
        </p>

        <p>
          I’m interested in where AI can be useful in engineering and
          software development, including its limitations. Getting code
          to run is a starting point; understanding its assumptions,
          checking its behavior, and deciding whether the result is
          useful are part of the work too.
        </p>

        <h2>What you’ll find here.</h2>

        <p>
          This site is my technical notebook: a place for aerospace
          studies, project write-ups, simulation experiments, Linux
          explorations, and analysis of hardware and software.
        </p>

        <p>
          Some posts will work through an engineering question in detail.
          Others will examine a new tool, compare approaches, or document
          something I tried. I want to show the reasoning behind the
          outcome, including what worked, what didn’t, and what I still
          need to understand.
        </p>

        <h2>Let’s connect.</h2>

        <p>
          If you’re working on something interesting in aerospace,
          engineering, or open technology, I’d be happy to hear about it.
        </p>

        {
          site.email && (
            <p>
              <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>Send me an email ↗</a>
            </p>
          )
        }

        {
          site.github && (
            <p>
              <a href={site.github}>GitHub ↗</a>
            </p>
          )
        }

        {
          site.linkedin && (
            <p>
              <a href={site.linkedin}>LinkedIn ↗</a>
            </p>
          )
        }
      </div>

      <aside class="profile-panel">
        <div class="eyebrow">CURRENT INTERESTS</div>

        <ul>
          <li>Aircraft design & blended-wing bodies</li>
          <li>Rocketry & flight simulation</li>
          <li>Manufacturing & practical design</li>
          <li>Python & scientific computing</li>
          <li>Fedora Linux & open-source tools</li>
          <li>AI-assisted programming</li>
          <li>Computer hardware & tech analysis</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="profile-bottom">
          <span class="status-dot"></span>
          Learning. Building. Iterating.
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>